The post-theorem introduction states explicitly that \emph{“Theorem \ref{T1} will be a consequence of Theorem \ref{T2A} and Theorem \ref{T2B}.”} In other words, to prove the main theorem, we combine the following two results.

We first use the scalar curvature average bound provided by
\begin{theorem}
\label{T2B}Let $(M,J,\theta)$ be a complete noncompact Sasakian $(2n+1)$-manifold with nonnegative transverse bisectional curvature and the maximal
volume growth. Then we have
\[\begin{array}
[c]{c}\frac{1}{B_{\xi}(x,r)}\int_{B_{\xi}(x,r)}R^{T}\omega^{n}\wedge\eta\leq\frac
{C}{1+r^{2}}\end{array}
\]
for all $r$, $x\in M$ and a positive constant $C$.
\end{theorem}

Then we apply the structural/topological conclusion given by
\begin{theorem}
\label{T2A}Let $(M,\xi,\eta,g,\Phi,\omega)$ be a complete noncompact Sasakian
$(2n+1)$-manifold with nonnegative transverse bisectional curvature. Suppose
that for some positive constants $C_{1},C_{2}$, we have the maximal volume
growth\[\begin{array}
[c]{c}\mathrm{Vol}(B_{\xi}(p,r))\geq C_{1}r^{2n},\text{ \ \textrm{for all} }r\text{
\textrm{and some }}p\in M
\end{array}
\]
and
\[\begin{array}
[c]{c}\frac{1}{\mathrm{Vol}(B_{\xi}(x,r))}\int_{B_{\xi}(x,r)}R^{T}\omega^{n}\wedge\eta\leq\frac{C_{2}}{(1+r)^{2}},\text{\textrm{ for all} }r,x\in M.
\end{array}
\]
Then

\begin{enumerate}
\item $M$ is CR-biholomorphic to $\Omega\times\mathbb{R},$ where $\Omega$ is a
pseuodoconvex domain in the $\mathbb{C}^{n}$.

\item $M$ is homeomorphic to $\mathbb{R}^{4}\times\mathbb{R}$ for $n=2$ and
$M$ is diffeomorphic to $\mathbb{R}^{2n}\times\mathbb{R}$ for $n>2.$
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

Thus, the auxiliary theorem above (Theorem \ref{T2B}) supplies the required average transverse scalar curvature decay estimate, and the preceding theorem (Theorem \ref{T2A}) then yields the CR-biholomorphism to $\Omega\times\mathbb{R}$ with $\Omega\subset\mathbb{C}^n$ pseudoconvex, along with the stated topological conclusions in dimensions $n=2$ and $n>2$. This completes the proof of the main theorem.

The post-theorem introduction outlines the following proof strategy (centered around the main theorem):

- The proofs are based on a key estimate showing that the moments of the Malliavin derivative of the solution of
\begin{align}
\label{HAM}
	\begin{cases}
		\dfrac{\partial^2 u}{\partial^2 t} (t,x)
		=  \dfrac{\partial^2 u}{\partial x^2} (t,x)+u(t,x) \dot{X}(t,x), \\
		t>0, \ x \in \bR, \\
		u(0,x) = 1, \dfrac{\partial u}{\partial t} (0,x)=0, \quad x \in \bR,
	\end{cases}
\end{align}
can be bounded, up to a constant, by a deterministic function, namely
\begin{equation}
\label{key-D}
\|D_{r,y,z}u(t,x)\|_p \leq C'_{T,p,\nu,\k}|z| \int_{\bR}G_{t-r}(x-y')\k(y-y')dy',
